Date: Sun, 2 Jan 1994 11:30:53 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Inner Cities and "Jurisdiction Shopping"

Especially in the context of nations, Duncan Frissell, Nick Szabo, and
others have mentioned "jurisdiction shopping" and "perpetual
tourists." People and companies move to nations which are less hostile
to their plans and which offer tax breaks or at least don't confiscate
their wealth.

A lot of the immigration into the U.S. is, ironically, this kind of
economic opportunity-seeking, as people flee from Haiti, Mexico, El
Salvador, and the like, to the increased opportunities for jobs in the
U.S. And some fraction of U.S. folks flee in the reverse direction to
the Bahamas, Switzerland, etc.

Nothing new here.

What I want to draw a parallel to is the flight from inner cities to
suburbs or to other cities with less-onerous burdens. This has been
happening for the past 40 years and is a major cause of the decay of
inner cities. Known crudely--but accurately--as "white flight," it
represents a concrete manifestation of the jurisdiction shopping that
several of us have talked about.

San Francisco is the example I'll talk about, not becuase it's the
best example of a decayed city (it's actually fairly healthy and
dynamic) but because I live in the greater Bay Area and have seen the
forces at work.

In a nutshell, here's what has happened:

- SF increased welfare, medical benefits, etc. to population (mostly
1960s)

- property taxes and business taxes were increased to pay for these
expanded city programs (no cities that I know of have separate income
taxes, so the brunt of the taxes are property taxes, special
assessments, permits and licenses, etc.).

- in the 1970s, companies begain exiting SF for the cheaper suburbs,
such as San Mateo, Pleasanton, Concord, Richmond, etc. (This has been
especially true for some large financial services companies--Wells
Fargo, Bank of America, Visa, etc.--which have moved most processing
to outlying areas.)

(This pattern was clearly seen in the 1970s with New York City, as
numerous companies moved their headquarters and/or main processing
facilities to the cheaper and less restrictive suburbs. The collapse
of real estate prices at that time was dramatic. Later, prices
recovered and even went up and the "revitalization" of NYC was
heralded. I have the impression--which Perry, Harry, Duncan, and
others can comment on--that stagnation and flight is again ocurring.)

- as the business base shrinks, a familiar cycle escalates: tax rates
on businesses are ratcheted up to compensate for the shrinkage, which
accelerates the flight, etc.

- so long as companies and individuals are free to desert the "high
tax" regions for lower tax regions, this effect continues. (The flight
from cities _does_ cause cities to somewhat moderate their tax
policies, of course, as the city government is well-aware of why
companies are leaving.)

- and the effects happen at the _state_ level as well, as companies
and individuals are free to move at will across state lines. Thus,
California is losing manufacturing sites to other states, for a
variety of reasons (high personal and corporate income tax, perceived
crime and social problems, tax breaks offered elsewhere, etc.).


Anyway, I mention this effect because it gives us clues about a major
way to break the power of the State: increase the ways in which the
"tax gradient" can drive individuals and corporations to cheaper
areas.

Telecommuting advances and new workgroup management tools may fairly
soon allow many more people to live and work from regions with less
statism.

This could trigger the "phase change" many of us see.

--Tim May
