Date: Sun, 2 Jan 1994 12:38:20 -0500 (EST)
From: "Harry S. Hawk" <habs@panix.com>
Subject: ECO: Japan as proof of free markets

Japan as proof of the free market working.

A debate that raged on this list perhaps a year ago, centered on if we
could ever have a scientific test of the free market. The debate
raised the issue, "could you have something like a double-blind
controlled study of markets." Since such a study seems unlikely a
deeper question remained, "is their anything equivalent to it?"

Perry Metzger made several points about this which I wish to echo and
amplify in light of recent economic developments in Japan. I can't
quote Perry directly but the gist of his remarks dealt with what would
be valid tests of economic theory. Roughly speaking, Mr. Metzger
indicated that the comparison of the economics of the (for example)
USSR and the United States is all the evidence one needs to see that
free markets work better, more of the time, than those markets that
are not free.

At the time of this debate, there were voices that thought such
evidence that Mr.  Metzger presented was not good enough. (Most of
these people have left the list, and I trust anyone on the list
harbors no doubts of the powerful force of free markets). Off the list
in our general society at large, there are still many who would doubt
the free market's power and especially would have you believe in
various tools of socialism -- managed competition and industrial
policy. Clearly I would count the President of The United States
amongst such promoter's of socialism.

Recently the popular press has been writing about the economic
downturn of Japan (often referred to as Japan, Inc.) I think the turn
of events surrounding this economic collapse is the capstone in any
proof that markets that have more freedom do better than markets that
are less free.

Japan has/had many great advantages:

Stable, strong, healthy, well educated, capital rich population. They
have had access to top technology, management practices, and world
markets.

They've also had many forms of managed trade and other forms of subtle
socialism guiding their economies. And they have "failed." I don't
count them down and out, and I expect them to make improvements that
will help them rebound. However, and this is the sole point this
message, I wish to tout their recent failures as proof that even minor
and subtle forms of socialism can ruin the strongest of economies.
Proof that if you want liberty and justice and equality and access to
capital for the greatest number of any population the only valid
approach is to harness the power of the free market.

And when we speak to our too PC neighbors who rant about how our
synthetic society is unnatural and that only stalwart governance can
bridle the destructive nature of man, we can, if we wish, point out
that the only "natural" way to prosperity is that of nature who by
empirical evidence likes His :) markets free.

/hawk

