Tim, this is at a tangent to your point.  Free, optimally coordinated 
markets don't need to clear though price controls will make sure 
they don't.  Non-price allocation (because using prices costs), 
sticky prices (restaurants usually have excess capacity)  and 
rational procrastination (lose by idling a resource to avoid losing 
much more later) mean that rationing can make sense.


